insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,fact,source_note) values
('1 Corinthians 13:13','Paul ends his description of love by naming faith, hope, and love together—and then says love is the greatest of the three.','1 Corinthians 13:13.'),
('1 Kings 18:31','Elijah built the altar from twelve stones, one for each tribe of Israel. Even in a divided kingdom, the altar symbolically remembered the whole people.','1 Kings 18:30–32.'),
('1 Samuel 16:11','Samuel was told there was still one son left: David was out tending sheep. The future king was the one not originally presented with his brothers.','1 Samuel 16:10–13.'),
('1 Samuel 17:49','David did not defeat Goliath with Saul''s armor. The account says one stone from his sling struck Goliath''s forehead.','1 Samuel 17:38–40,49.'),
('2 Chronicles 32:3','Before the Assyrian attack, Hezekiah consulted others about stopping water from the springs outside Jerusalem. The king did not treat planning as a lack of faith.','2 Chronicles 32:2–4.'),
('2 Chronicles 36:22','The book ends with Cyrus calling for God''s people to go up to Jerusalem and build. Its final words open a path toward a new beginning.','2 Chronicles 36:22–23.'),
('2 Kings 18:14','Hezekiah sent a message to the Assyrian king at Lachish and accepted a payment demand. The narrative does not hide the pressure Judah faced.','2 Kings 18:13–16.'),
('2 Kings 20:7','Isaiah ordered a cake of figs placed on Hezekiah''s boil. The healing account includes a practical treatment alongside the prophetic message.','2 Kings 20:1–7.'),
('2 Kings 9:3','The young prophet was instructed to pour oil on Jehu''s head before announcing his appointment as king. The ceremony came before the political action.','2 Kings 9:1–6.'),
('2 Timothy 4:11','Paul says only Luke is with him and asks for Mark to come because he is useful in ministry. Relationships and teamwork matter even near the end of the letter.','2 Timothy 4:11.'),
('Acts 17:16','Paul''s speech in Athens began with his concern at seeing the city full of idols. His message grew out of paying attention to the place he was visiting.','Acts 17:16–23.'),
('Acts 21:27','The uproar about Paul began when people from Asia saw him in the Temple and stirred the crowd. The accusation was not a finding of a formal trial.','Acts 21:27–29.'),
('Acts 9:17','Ananias addressed the recently blinded Saul as ''Brother Saul'' before his sight returned. The greeting makes the meeting more personal than a simple medical event.','Acts 9:17–18.'),
('Colossians 4:14','Luke is called ''the beloved physician'' in Paul''s closing greetings. The New Testament preserves this detail about one of Paul''s companions.','Colossians 4:14.'),
('Daniel 5:25','The writing on the wall contained just four words: Mene, Mene, Tekel, Parsin. Daniel then explained their message to the king.','Daniel 5:25–28.'),
('Deuteronomy 8:17','Moses warns Israel not to say, ''My power and the might of my hand have gained me this wealth.'' The warning comes amid a description of future prosperity.','Deuteronomy 8:11–18.'),
('Esther 9:22','The celebration in Esther included sending food to one another and giving gifts to the poor. The feast was meant to be shared.','Esther 9:20–22.'),
('Exodus 14:13','Moses told the frightened people to stand firm and see God''s salvation. The command comes while the sea is still ahead of them.','Exodus 14:10–14.'),
('Exodus 15:27','After the bitter water at Marah, Israel reached Elim, where there were twelve springs and seventy palm trees. The journey included an oasis.','Exodus 15:23–27.'),
('Exodus 2:10','Moses'' name is explained in the story by Pharaoh''s daughter drawing him out of the water. The naming points back to his rescue.','Exodus 2:10.'),
('Exodus 25:31','The lampstand was to be hammered from pure gold, including its cups, buds, and blossoms. The details of the sanctuary included craftsmanship.','Exodus 25:31–40.'),
('Exodus 32:19','Moses broke the tablets when he saw the calf and the dancing. The act happens after the covenant instructions have been written down.','Exodus 32:15–19.'),
('Ezra 1:7','Cyrus returned articles taken from Jerusalem''s Temple by Nebuchadnezzar. The return from exile involved physical objects as well as people.','Ezra 1:7–11.'),
('Galatians 5:13','Paul warns that freedom must not become an excuse to indulge the flesh. He immediately connects freedom with serving one another in love.','Galatians 5:13–14.'),
('Genesis 11:9','Genesis connects the name Babel with the confusion of language. The story''s wordplay emphasizes the very division the builders hoped to avoid.','Genesis 11:9; biblical Hebrew explanation of Babel.'),
('Genesis 12:3','The promise to Abram extends beyond his own descendants: Genesis says all the families of the earth will be blessed through him.','Genesis 12:2–3.'),
('Genesis 14:18','Melchizedek is introduced as both king of Salem and priest of God Most High. Hebrews later returns to this unusual combination.','Genesis 14:18–20; Hebrews 7:1–3.'),
('Genesis 22:13','When Abraham looked up, he saw a ram caught in a thicket. The ram was offered instead of Isaac, making provision part of the story''s turning point.','Genesis 22:11–14.'),
('Genesis 23:2','Sarah died at Kiriath Arba, which Genesis identifies as Hebron. The verse preserves both the old place name and its identification.','Genesis 23:2.'),
('Genesis 24:19','Rebekah did not stop after giving Abraham''s servant water. She volunteered to draw water for the camels too, a much larger act of hospitality.','Genesis 24:17–20.'),
('Genesis 32:30','Jacob named the place Peniel because he said he had seen God face to face and survived. The place name itself becomes part of the testimony.','Genesis 32:30–31; Hebrew Peniel/Penuel.'),
('Genesis 37:9','Joseph''s second dream involved the sun, moon, and eleven stars bowing to him. The dream used symbols that his family understood personally.','Genesis 37:9–11.'),
('Genesis 4:15','After Cain''s crime, God still placed a protective sign on him to prevent immediate revenge. Judgment and restraint appear in the same account.','Genesis 4:13–15.'),
('Genesis 41:16','When Pharaoh asked Joseph to interpret dreams, Joseph answered that the interpretation would come from God, not from his own brilliance.','Genesis 41:15–16.'),
('Genesis 5:27','Methuselah''s recorded age is 969 years, the longest lifespan listed in Genesis. The number belongs to the Bible''s genealogy, not an independently verified modern record.','Genesis 5:25–27.'),
('Genesis 50:20','Joseph told his brothers they intended evil, but God used the events for good and for preserving many lives. He did not pretend their actions were harmless.','Genesis 50:19–21.'),
('Genesis 6:18','God promises to establish His covenant with Noah before giving the family instructions for entering the ark. The word covenant appears in the rescue account.','Genesis 6:17–22.'),
('Genesis 7:16','After the family and animals entered, Genesis says the Lord shut Noah in. The final act of closing the ark is attributed to God.','Genesis 7:13–16.'),
('Genesis 8:11','The dove returned with a freshly picked olive leaf. That small detail is the sign Noah received that the waters had receded.','Genesis 8:10–12.'),
('Habakkuk 2:2','Habakkuk is told to write the vision plainly on tablets. The prophetic instruction includes making the message understandable to readers.','Habakkuk 2:2–3.'),
('Hosea 11:3','God speaks of teaching Ephraim to walk and taking Israel in His arms. The prophetic picture uses the closeness of a parent caring for a child.','Hosea 11:1–4.'),
('Isaiah 44:3','Isaiah compares God''s promised blessing to water on thirsty ground, then speaks of pouring His Spirit on descendants. Two pictures unfold together.','Isaiah 44:3–5.'),
('Isaiah 7:3','When Isaiah was sent to meet King Ahaz, he was told to take his son Shear-jashub along. Even the child''s Hebrew name carried a message about a remnant returning.','Isaiah 7:3; Hebrew Shear-jashub.'),
('Jeremiah 36:23','The king cut Jeremiah''s scroll with a knife and threw the pieces into a fire. The chapter later tells how the words were written again.','Jeremiah 36:21–23,27–32.'),
('Joel 2:13','Joel urges the people to tear their hearts rather than only their clothes. The prophet contrasts outward signs of grief with sincere repentance.','Joel 2:12–13.'),
('John 19:23','The soldiers divided Jesus'' garments, but His tunic was woven in one piece. They chose to cast lots instead of tearing it.','John 19:23–24.'),
('John 5:8','Jesus told the man by the pool to rise, pick up his mat, and walk. The instruction gave him something to do immediately after years of waiting.','John 5:5–9.'),
('Jonah 1:3','Jonah tried to sail toward Tarshish after being sent to Nineveh. His planned route is the opposite direction of the assignment as the story presents it.','Jonah 1:1–3.'),
('Joshua 10:11','Joshua says large hailstones struck the opposing forces. The account names the storm as a major part of the battle, not just Israel''s weapons.','Joshua 10:11.'),
('Joshua 3:15','The Jordan was overflowing its banks at harvest time when the priests approached it. The crossing was not described as happening at the easiest season.','Joshua 3:14–17.'),
('Leviticus 25:23','The land law says the land ultimately belongs to God and the Israelites are sojourners with Him. Ownership is presented as a stewardship.','Leviticus 25:23–24.'),
('Luke 3:23','Luke says Jesus was about thirty years old when He began His ministry. The number is given as an approximation, not an exact birth date.','Luke 3:23.'),
('Mark 1:35','Jesus rose very early while it was still dark and went to a solitary place to pray, even during a busy period of healing and teaching.','Mark 1:32–39.'),
('Matthew 2:11','When the visitors from the east reached Jesus, Matthew says they entered a house. The text does not place their visit at the manger scene.','Matthew 2:1–11; compare Luke 2:7.'),
('Matthew 27:51','Matthew reports the Temple curtain tearing from top to bottom as Jesus died. The detail is linked directly to the crucifixion scene.','Matthew 27:50–51.'),
('Nahum 3:19','The book ends with the declaration that Nineveh''s wound cannot be healed. The prophetic conclusion addresses an empire once feared by nations.','Nahum 3:18–19.'),
('Nehemiah 6:3','Nehemiah refused to abandon the wall-building work to attend his opponents'' meeting. His response was that he was doing a great work and could not come down.','Nehemiah 6:1–4.'),
('Numbers 24:2','Before Balaam''s oracle, Numbers says the Spirit of God came upon him as he looked toward Israel''s camp. The scene precedes the star-and-sceptre saying.','Numbers 24:1–4,17.'),
('Numbers 6:5','A Nazirite''s vow included leaving the hair uncut during the period of dedication. The visible practice was part of a broader commitment.','Numbers 6:1–5.'),
('Philippians 4:12','Paul says he learned contentment both when he had plenty and when he was in need. His teaching is not limited to surviving poverty.','Philippians 4:11–13.'),
('Proverbs 25:11','The proverb compares a fittingly spoken word to golden apples in silver settings. Wisdom is described with the image of something carefully made.','Proverbs 25:11.'),
('Psalm 119:11','The psalmist says he has stored God''s word in his heart so he might not sin. Memory is presented as a way to live wisely, not merely to win a contest.','Psalm 119:9–11.'),
('Revelation 22:17','Near the Bible''s end, the Spirit and the Bride say ''Come.'' The invitation expands to anyone who is thirsty and wants the water of life.','Revelation 22:17.'),
('Ruth 4:17','The women name Ruth''s child Obed. The genealogy identifies Obed as Jesse''s father and David''s grandfather, connecting an ordinary family story to Israel''s kingship.','Ruth 4:13–17.'),
('Zechariah 9:10','Right after the king''s arrival on a donkey, the vision speaks of ending battle weapons and proclaiming peace to the nations.','Zechariah 9:9–10.')
